-> HAS_FACET [node:a242881f-81a0-4faa-9c5c-e87c1fbb5926] @ 2026-07-24T10:52:43.563572+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:b4448ae8-c0d3-4aca-a3ad-8598096b957a] @ 2026-07-24T10:52:43.563572+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:efd6891c-e4f2-4281-a885-47aec24cf34f] @ 2026-07-24T10:52:43.563572+00:00

Generalizable review finding: a first-writer-wins / INSERT-OR-IGNORE persistence scheme keyed on (entity, dimension) that also carries a provenance/source tag protects only the write ORDER it was designed for. In coffer-classification, saveAssignments() (sqlite-classification-store.adapter.ts + in-memory fake) makes 'first assignment written for a (tx,group) pair wins, regardless of source' -- this correctly makes a LATER rule re-eval never clobber an EARLIER manual correction (sticky-manual, tested). But the converse is untested and silently wrong: if the engine already wrote a source:'rule' row for (tx,group), and a user later manually assigns tx to that SAME group (Container.assign() has no guard), the manual write is silently ignored -- the row stays source:'rule', not promoted to source:'manual'. Provenance then understates user intent: if the rule is later edited/removed, this group assignment is not protected as a correction should be. General lesson: when reviewing any first-writer-wins/upsert-with-provenance design, test BOTH write orders, not just the one the invariant name advertises.
---
