coffer-classification (slice 2 of coffer-mvp) delivered, reviewed, GREEN: Group/Tag tree model with cycle-guard; Rule model with predicate-as-DATA (discriminated union, all/any combinators) evaluated by a PURE core engine (classify(txns, rules)) that accumulates the ADDITIVE UNION of every matching rule's assign groups per tx (never first-match), honoring stopAfter as a per-tx exclusivity escape; a NEW ClassificationStorePort (not a StorePort extension) persists groups/rules/assignments behind a SQLite adapter (2nd connection to the same db file, real FK assignments->transactions, PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON + busy_timeout=5000 verified by a dedicated throwing test) and an in-memory fake sharing one contract suite; assignments carry provenance (source: rule|manual|assist) and are sticky via first-writer-wins INSERT OR IGNORE on (tx_content_hash, group_id) -- protects manual-then-rule ordering (tested), but the reverse order (manual write after an existing rule row for the same pair) is a known, captured gap, see the sibling issue node. Review queue is a derived read (zero-assignment txns), never a persisted table. Correction-to-rule (promote.ts derivePredicate + correction.ts orchestration) mints a rule from a manual correction's tx (counterparty-equals default, description-equals fallback) without auto-reclassifying -- caller re-runs classify() so the new rule applies uniformly. AssistPort ships a heuristic adapter (token-overlap + frequency ranking, zero store/network imports) and a network-free LLM transport-stub adapter, both gated off by default at the composition root (config.assist.enabled, checked BEFORE building the assist context, not just inside the adapter) -- no network on any pnpm path. Container wires classificationStore + assist and exposes classify/reviewQueue/assign/promoteToRule/suggest. Verified independently at review (not just on trust): read+ran the sticky-manual contract test, the FK-enforcement throwing test, boundary-lint plus an independent grep of core/ports imports (clean), and the assist-never-commits import-scan tests. pnpm typecheck (452 files/0 errors) && pnpm test (240 passed/1 skipped) && pnpm build all green at review. Verdict: Approve, with the provenance-asymmetry issue carried forward as a backlog item, not a blocker (narrow write-order edge case, no data loss). Why durable for slice 3 (coffer-analytics): the many-to-many invariant, the additive-union engine semantics, and the review-queue-is-derived pattern are the exact shape analytics' attribution modes (overlap vs partition) must build on without assuming one-group-per-tx.
---
