gw-review finding (coffer-ui-i18n, generalizable): a plan-stated fallback TRIPWIRE phrased as a verified-failure condition ('if X fails to mount / peers refuse Y') can get satisfied by a preemptive risk judgment instead of an actual observed failure, and still get 'recorded honestly' in a code comment — but without a captured memory node, so the graph shows the ORIGINAL tripwire wording as still-accurate when the actual trigger was weaker (time-boxed risk avoidance, not a reproduced mount failure). Concretely: [node:167451f0]'s layerchart tripwire said 'if layerchart FAILS to mount in jsdom'; CashflowChart.svelte/ByGroupChart.svelte's header comments say layerchart 2.0.2 'was NOT wired' because its Svelte-5 mounting story was 'unverified... within this phase's time-box' — a narrower, more honest claim than the plan's literal criterion, but never captured as its own decision/issue node narrowing 167451f0. Lesson: when an implementer takes a tripwire's fallback branch on a softer trigger than the plan specified, capture that as a narrowing decision node (like ac2535ce narrowed bc0ab42f) — not just an in-code comment — so a future recall doesn't read the tripwire condition as having been met exactly as written.
